  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry and seasonable through Tue night with gusty\nNE winds developing along the coast. Lows tonight and Tue \nnight mainly in the 30s and 40s, with afternoon highs Tue in the\nupper 50s-middle 60s.\n\nDry and seasonable weather continues through Tue night. Large\nhigh pressure across eastern Canada coupled with low pressure \noff the North Carolina coast will result in an increasing\npressure gradient. This will result in a N-NE winds increasing\nTue into Tue night along the coastal plain. Northeast wind gusts \nof 20-30 mph are expected to develop along the I-95 corridor\nwith 30-40 mph wind gusts for the Cape and Islands. In fact,\nNantucket may see a few gusts on the order of 45+ mph and\napproach marginal Wind Advisory criteria.\n\nWe expect dry weather to prevail through Tue night outside the\nlow risk for a few showers reaching Nantucket. We may see some\nlow clouds at times develop towards the southeast New England\ncoast, but the greater risk for eastern MA/RI may wait until \nvery late Tue night. \n\nLow temps tonight and Tue night will be mainly in the 30s and\n40s. The mildest lows will be across the Cape/Islands, where\ngusty NE winds will hold overnight low temps in the middle 40s\nto the lower 50s. High temperatures on Tuesday will be in the \nupper 50s to the middle 60s. The warmest of those highs will be \nacross the interior with the coolest temps will be towards the \nCape and Islands given the marine influence.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Chances for rainfall towards coastal areas\nWednesday into Thursday due to an offshore nor'easter well to\nour south. Main impacts from this system will be marine-based: \nrough seas, beach erosion, and strong winds.\n\nConfidence remains high that an offshore nor'easter will remain\nmainly south of our region. The questions are: how close does \nit approach southern New England? And how quickly will it move \npast us?\n\nThe NAM continues to be the farthest west of most of the\nguidance. The majority of solutions keep this low pressure well\nsouth of the 40N/70W benchmark. There are large differences in\ntiming, with the GFS on the slower end, and the ECMWF the most\nprogressive. All things considered, there is a risk for showers\nacross southeast MA and perhaps part of eastern RI sometime from\nWednesday afternoon into Thursday morning. Most of southern New\nEngland will remain dry during this time. Total rainfall should\nbe light, with perhaps Nantucket seeing around one quarter inch.\n\nThe main impacts from this nor'easter will be over the coastal\nmarine areas. NE gales continue to be expected across the\nsouthern waters, as well as rough seas. Rough seas also expected\nacross the eastern waters, but will not be quite as high.\nPersistent easterly fetch associated with these winds, along \nwith building rough seas, will need to be monitored as well, as \nbeach erosion along east- and northeast-facing beaches may \nbecome an issue.",
